d  dating back as far as 1999 he remembers  seeing about 30 patients a day the  average i see is anywhere from 50 to 60  patients a day every time i'm in the  office now he's so busy new patients  can't see him until march of next year  the day before we visited him he had  diagnosed a man with tongue cancer that  patient waited three months to see him  the longer people wait their diagnosis  gets laid and so things like cancer and  other illnesses you want to treat them  early so that patient will have a better  chance of cure dr carlos miato is the  island's health secretary i had a woman  write to me and say that her father was  having a stroke  and they couldn't find a neurosurgeon  they couldn't find one  and that's why people are also leaving  the islands i have 17. never surgery  so if you have 17 neurosurgeons  and how many people live here  3.2 billion hey siri what's 3.2 million  divided by 17  two hundred thousand divided by  seventeen is approximately one hundred  eighty eight thousand two hundred thirty  five point two nine so you have one  neurosurgeon for every one hundred and  eighty eight thousand people yeah  that's scary it is nicole damiani's 25  year old husband carlos rivera was  hospitalized about two and a half weeks  ago what happened to carlos so he sat up  on his bed and he fell onto the floor  and he was having a seizure and so i  rushed him to the hospital  and there was something on his ct scan  how many days before you saw a  neurosurgeon eight  eight days did they tell you why  well the doctors are overworked  there are many many cases patients  people that need help so  the fact that there's you know of  limited amount of practitioners  certified practitioners  available to attend the masses is pretty  alarming he had bleeding and swelling on  his brain he spoke to us from his  hospital bed in cagos puerto rico it's  really hard to find a nerdy surgeon here  in puerto rico and he got to a point  where i really  low-key gave up in life  and like it got really frustrating to a  point now one of the reasons patients  like carlos have such a hard time  getting medical care is that many puerto  rican doctors are moving to florida  because the pay is significantly better  for instance the average yearly salary  for a family physician in florida is two  hundred and twenty one thousand dollars  in puerto rico they make less than one  hundred thousand  jorge mata runs centro medico it's a  complex of hospitals in san juan he took  us to the icu to witness the impact of  low wages this is one of the empty rooms  that  if we were to have uh nurses it would be  used  as you see it's completely  ready  15 icu beds  eight of them are empty because they  don't have the nurses to work them  galia malias has been a registered nurse  at central medical for 19 years  she rides her son's bicycle to work she  says her pay is so low she can't afford  a car every 15 days you were telling me  your check is how much  eight hundred  ninety one dollars  is like a 25 cents or something like  that  that's not enough to live on for you no  that's not enough i need to move to  puerto rico  i have to learn  more english  because i have two ministers you have  two masters yeah wow and you're making  less than 900 every 15 days yeah  i love my job  i love my country but i don't see any in  this in this  country  i'm very sad for that very very sad  because my mom made me  and my son  these young men and women are the future  doctors from puerto rico and they showed  up as a group to let us know how gravely  concerned they are about the state of  health care on the island carlo bocas is  a second year medical student at the  university of puerto rico we look at the  conditions around us the things that  doctors tell us our own professors  family members that may be in medicine  we hear constantly about the problems  that are facing the island and just  within three years we might have to make  that faithful decision where we eit